Enough talk. Let's do this.

*cracks knuckles*

Looking at those tests, I see what needs punching. Cart with items, quantities, totals. Simple. Direct. No fancy footwork needed.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			if quantity <= 0 {
				c.RemoveItem(name)
				return
			}
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

*punches fist into palm*

That's it. Cart holds items. Add 'em, remove 'em, count 'em, clear 'em. Total's just price times quantity, summed up. When you add the same item twice, quantities stack - makes sense, like landing two punches on the same spot.

UpdateQuantity handles the edge case - set to zero or less, item's gone. Just like a problem I've punched hard enough.

No validation beyond what the tests demand. No panic handling. No thread safety. Tests didn't ask for those, so I didn't build them. That's not laziness - that's discipline. You build what you need, not what you imagine you might need someday.

I punch problems. They get solved.
